Recon:
1) Find Devices in Network - ping from network/target space
2) Find open ports on live devices - For each live device found, run an nmap or nc scan from your ops box through a dynamic tunnel to identify open ports
3) Identify running services - From your ops box and through dynamic tunnels, nmap using -sV
4) Interrogate/enumerate services with appropriate NSE scripts such as http-enum.nse or smb-os-discovery.nse



Web Explotation
Once we find ports/services:


FTP:
- wget -r (it is possible to provide credentials if we have them to see if there are additional resources an anonymous login won't retrieve)
-- Be Sure to look at everything you pull back
-- Consider running an ftp-enum script against the FTP port for additional information


ssh/telnet:
- If we have creds/keys, we can attempt to authenticate onto the box


websites:
- run a commandline dig or whois against the IP or url (don't use browser based dig or whois)
- view all pages you find (open links in different browser tabs so you don't forget to check any)
-- View page source code and search for href to see if there are any links you may have missed to other sites
-- check robots.txt for additional pages and directories and open those in new tabs
-- check any sites and directories indicated by the http-enum script and open those in new tabs


Website input boxes:
- attempt directory traversal (../../../../../../etc/passwd)
- attempt command injection (; whoami)
- check for places we can upload malicious files
-- If we can upload, we can potentially upload a command injection script (see student FG)
-- In order for us to use malicious uploads, we need to be able to access the uploaded file. If we cannot access uploads, we cannot use malicious uploads to gain access
- If there's a message board, chat service, or similar, attempt xss such as the cookie stealer script
** BE MINDFUL OF POST VS GET. POST will not change what is in the URL bar. GET will often show ".php?[variable]=[value]"


SQL Injection:
- remember ; ends a command and is useful especially in POST requests, though not always required
- ending a line with # or -- comments out the rest of the select statement, preventing it from executing

Logins:
For POST requests (input boxes that do not show in the URL):
- attempt [tom' OR 1='1] in username and password boxes to see if we get something useful back.
-- We can go into Developer Options (F12) to view the POST request and see what the used variables are called
For GET requests (when our input shows up in the URL following php?):
- identify the username and password variable names in the php
- change variables in the URL to "tom OR 1=1" (quotation marks not needed)

Database queries:
For POST requests:
1) identify vulnerable fields by inputting [<option>' OR 1='1] to see if we get more results back than we should
2) identify how many columns the vulnerable query requires by typing [<vulnerable option> UNION SELECT 1,2,3...], adding an additional column until we get results.
-- It is recommended to start with the number of columns the legitimate query shows you
-- If it gives you an error, add another column until you see the numbers at the bottom of the results
-- Take note of any numbers that don't display or whose format changes (becomes a decimal). Be mindful of any data we put in those columns.
3) dump the database with our Golden Statement
-- [<vulnerable option> UNION SELECT table_schema,table_name,column_name[4,5,...] FROM information_schema.columns; --]
4) interrogate any discovered user created databases and tables
-- [<vulnerable option> UNION SELECT <column1,column2,column3...> from <database>.<table>; --]

For GET requests:
1) Determine what a query looks like and looking for the ".php?<variable>=<value>"
2) identify vulnerable entry by inputting "php?<variable>=<option> OR 1=1" to see if we get more results back than we should
3) identify how many columns the vulnerable query requires by typing "php?<variable>=<vulnerable option> UNION SELECT 1,2,3...", adding an additional column until we get results.
-- It is recommended to start with the number of columns the legitimate query sh
ows you
-- If it gives you an error, add another column until you see the numbers at the bottom of the results
-- Take note of any numbers that don't display or whose format changes (becomes a decimal). Be mindful of any data we put in those columns.
4) dump the database with our Golden Statement
-- "php?<variable>=<vulnerable option> UNION SELECT table_schema,table_name,column_name(4,5,...) FROM information_schema.columns "
-- information_schema, mysql, and performance_schema are default databases and not usually of interest of us
5) interrogate any discovered user created databases and tables
-- "php?<variable>=<vulnerable option> UNION SELECT <column1,column22,column3...> from <database>.<table>



Post-Exploitation:
Enumerate other users:
	Nix:
		- cat /etc/passwd
		- cat /etc/group
		- view /home/ directory and check user directories if able
		- w or who to see who is currently online
	Win:
		- net user
		- net localgroup
		- query user
		- view C:\Users\ directory and check user directories if able
Enumerate processes and services of interest:
	Nix:
		- ps -elfH
		- systemctl list-units --type service OR service --status-all
		- check configs (usually in /etc/)
	Win: 
		- tasklist /v OR task manager
		- net start OR services manager
Enumerate Network Info:
	Nix:
		- identify ip/network info with ip a
		- identify current connections and listening services with ss -nltp
		- check /etc/hosts for possible hosts or networks
			Identified hosts and networks should be subjected to the recon steps above)
	Win: 
		- identify ip/network info with ipconfig /all
		- identify current connections and listening services with netstat /anob
Enumerate scheduled processes:
	Nix:
		- check /etc/crontab
		- check /etc/cron.d/*
		- check /var/spool/cron* and /var/spool/at
	Win:
		- Check schtasks or the Task Scheduler
Enumerate other files and directories of interest:
	Nix:
		- Any ssh keys or password files?
		- Anything interesting in /etc/?
		- uname -a or cat /etc/*rel*
		- Check /tmp and /var/tmp
		- syslog or rsyslog configs to see what will log
		- Check /var/log/
		- find / -type [f for file or d for directory] -iname "what you're looking for"
		- Check /root (once priv esc'd)
		- check /etc/shadow (once priv esc'd)
	Win: 
		- C:\Windows\temp
		- auditpol to see what will log
		- wevtutil or windows event viewer to view logs
		- [HKLM || HKCU]\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\[Run || RunOnce]
